    id: "lesson_big_lakes_to_the_sea", title: "The Big Lakes Go to the Sea", gradeLevel: "K-3", topic: "Geography", estimatedMinutes: 35, readingTimeMinutes: 3,
    description: "Water runs downhill. Follow a drop of water from Lake Superior through Lake Huron, Lake Erie, over Niagara Falls, across Lake Ontario and down the St. Lawrence River to the ocean.",
    heroImageUrl: IMG.niagara,
    images: [
      { url: IMG.superiorHero, caption: "Lake Superior, the biggest and highest of the Great Lakes", credit: "Wikimedia Commons" },
      { url: IMG.huronHero, caption: "Lake Huron", credit: "Wikimedia Commons" },
      { url: IMG.erieHero, caption: "Lake Erie, the shallowest", credit: "Wikimedia Commons" },
      { url: IMG.ontarioHero, caption: "Lake Ontario, the last lake before the river", credit: "Wikimedia Commons" },
      { url: IMG.stLawrenceGallery1, caption: "The St. Lawrence River carries all the lakes' water to the sea", credit: "Wikimedia Commons" },
    ],
    narrativeContent: `## Water runs downhill

Pour water on a slide. It runs down. Rivers do the same thing. They always run downhill to a bigger water, and in the end, to the sea.

## A drop of water takes a trip

Imagine one drop of water in **Lake Superior**. It is the biggest lake and the highest up. The drop flows out and into **Lake Huron**. Then it goes on into **Lake Erie**.

At the end of Lake Erie the drop falls over **Niagara Falls**! It is a long way down. Now it is in **Lake Ontario**.

From Lake Ontario the drop flows into the **St. Lawrence River**. The river is wide and long. At last the drop reaches the **Gulf of St. Lawrence** and the **Atlantic Ocean**.

## The lakes are connected

The five Great Lakes are like steps on a staircase. Water goes down the steps one by one. That is why a boat, or a canoe long ago, could travel from the middle of the country all the way to the ocean.`,
    introduction: "Pour a cup of water onto a tilted tray and watch it run to the low corner. Ask: 'Where does the water in our lake or river go?' Then show the Great Lakes on the map as a staircase going down to the sea.",
    mainContent: `## The chain
Lake Superior → Lake Huron → Lake Erie → Niagara River and Niagara Falls → Lake Ontario → St. Lawrence River → Gulf of St. Lawrence → Atlantic Ocean. (Lake Michigan joins Lake Huron from the side.)

## Facts that fit the band
- Lake Superior is the biggest and sits highest; Lake Ontario is the lowest.
- Niagara Falls is the big drop between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario, about as tall as a 15-storey building.
- The St. Lawrence River is the road from the lakes to the sea; Cartier sailed up it in 1535 and Champlain built Quebec on it in 1608.

## Three ideas for young learners
1. Water always runs downhill.
2. The Great Lakes are connected like steps.
3. Rivers join lakes to the ocean.`,
    objectives: ["Say that water flows downhill from lake to lake to the sea", "Put the five Great Lakes and the St. Lawrence River in order from highest to the ocean", "Name Niagara Falls as the big drop between two lakes", "Find Lake Superior and the Atlantic Ocean on a map"],
