    narrativeContent: `## Gold on Rabbit Creek

In August 1896 a Tagish man named Keish, known to the newcomers as Skookum Jim, his nephew Káa Goox (Dawson Charlie), his sister Shaaw Tláa (Kate Carmack) and her husband George Carmack were fishing and prospecting near the Klondike River. On a small stream called Rabbit Creek they found gold lying thick in the gravel. They renamed it Bonanza Creek. Through the winter the news stayed in the Yukon, and Shaaw Tláa's sewing and baking kept the camp going.

## The world finds out

In July 1897 two ships reached Seattle and San Francisco carrying miners and more than a tonne of gold. Newspapers shouted the story. Within months, about 100,000 people set out for the Klondike. Only around 30,000 made it.

## Over the pass

Most climbed the Chilkoot Pass from the coast. The last stretch was a staircase cut in the ice, the Golden Stairs, about 1,500 steps. At the top, the North-West Mounted Police checked every traveller. Nobody could enter Canada without a year's supplies, close to a tonne of food and gear. People climbed the stairs again and again, carrying it up a load at a time.

## Boats on the lake

On the far side lay Bennett Lake. Thousands of people camped there through the winter of 1897-98 and sawed trees into boats. When the ice went out on 29 May 1898, about 7,000 boats set off down the Yukon River. Miles Canyon and the White Horse rapids wrecked so many that Superintendent Sam Steele of the Mounted Police ordered that only skilled pilots could take boats through.

## Dawson, and Moosehide

The boats landed at Dawson City, built on the flat where the Klondike meets the Yukon. That flat was Tr'ochëk, a fishing camp of the Tr'ondëk Hwëch'in, the Hän people. In 1897 Chief Isaac led his people a few kilometres downriver to Moosehide, away from the crowd. Dawson grew to about 30,000 people, and in June 1898 the Yukon became its own territory. By 1899 the gold was mostly claimed, new strikes called from Alaska, and the stampede was over.`,
