    mainContent: `## Numbers for the board
- 16-17 August 1896: discovery on Bonanza Creek.
- July 1897: the gold ships reach Seattle and San Francisco; the stampede begins.
- About 100,000 set out; about 30,000 reach Dawson.
- One year of supplies required at the border: close to a tonne per person.
- 29 May 1898: the ice goes out at Bennett; about 7,000 boats.
- 13 June 1898: the Yukon Territory is created.

## The people already there
The Tr'ondëk Hwëch'in fished the Klondike mouth at Tr'ochëk every summer. The stampede took their camp. Chief Isaac moved the community to Moosehide in 1897 and kept its ceremonies and songs safe with relatives in Alaska. Today the Tr'ondëk Hwëch'in are a self-governing First Nation and Tr'ochëk is a national historic site.

## The discoverers
Keish (Skookum Jim), Káa Goox (Dawson Charlie), Shaaw Tláa (Kate Carmack) and George Carmack filed the first claims. Carmack's name went on the discovery claim; the family's part in the find is now on the record.`,
